# Generated Spring Backend

Backend generated by the UML Design Tool codegen (Java 21, Spring Boot 3.3.x,
Spring Data JPA). Entities, repositories, thin services, REST CRUD controllers,
relation mappings and this build/config bundle come straight from the diagram.

## Run locally (default profile — H2 file database)

No external services needed; data persists under ./data:

    ./mvnw spring-boot:run

On Windows use mvnw.cmd. The API is then served on port 8080 under /api/...

## Run against PostgreSQL (prod profile)

The prod profile reads everything from environment variables — nothing is
hardcoded in src/main/resources/application-prod.properties:

    export SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL=jdbc:postgresql://<host>:5432/<db>
    export SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME=<user>
    export SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD=<password>
    export SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod
    ./mvnw spring-boot:run

Or start the whole stack (app + PostgreSQL 16 with a persistent volume):

    docker compose up --build

## Deploy to AWS (EC2 + RDS PostgreSQL)

Production deployment itself is operator work; the generated project is ready
for it:

1. Create an RDS PostgreSQL instance and a database/user for this app.
2. Run the container built from the generated Dockerfile on an EC2 host
   (ECS/Elastic Beanstalk work the same way), setting the three
   SPRING_DATASOURCE_* variables plus SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod to point at
   the RDS endpoint.
3. The schema is managed by JPA (ddl-auto=update); switch to Flyway/liquibase
   if your team requires versioned migrations.

## Project layout

- src/main/java — entities, repositories, services (thin 3+1 layer), controllers
- src/main/resources — application.properties (H2 default) + application-prod.properties (PostgreSQL)
- pom.xml + Maven wrapper — ./mvnw works without a local Maven install
- postman_collection.json — colección de pruebas automáticas para todos los endpoints

## Pruebas de Endpoints con Postman

El proyecto incluye el archivo `postman_collection.json` en la raíz.
Para ejecutar las pruebas automáticas:
1. Abrir Postman e importar `postman_collection.json`.
2. Verificar que la variable `baseUrl` apunte a `http://localhost:8080`.
3. Hacer clic derecho en la colección y seleccionar **Run collection**.
4. Postman ejecutará todos los endpoints (CRUDs por entidad, Asistente y Sincronización Offline) y validará los códigos de respuesta con tests automáticos integrados.

